<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Pernicious anemia on weekly cyanocobalamin (D51.0)</content>. Hemoglobin up from 8.2 to 12.0 g/dL with MCV falling from 118 to 101 fL at five weeks; potassium normal. [S]<list><item>Continue weekly injections.</item><item>Eight-week review with the blood count, methylmalonic acid, homocysteine and iron studies, and the biopsy results.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Subacute combined degeneration (G32.0)</content>. Early recovery of joint position sense and balance. [S]<list><item>Continue physical therapy and the home balance exercises.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Blister on the left forefoot (S90.822A)</content>. Healed. [S]<list><item>Extra-depth shoes with toe fillers; daily checks.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Symptoms: fatigue, breathlessness, exercise tolerance, sore tongue, numbness</td><td>Every visit during active treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Energy and breathing better; tingling less</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Blood count with MCV</td><td>About eight weeks after starting replacement, with earlier checks in severe anemia</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Hemoglobin 12.0 g/dL, MCV 101 fL, white cells 5.6, platelets 232; full check due at eight weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Reticulocyte count</td><td>About one week after the first injection</td><td>Completed today</td><td>1.6%, settling after the peak</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Potassium</td><td>In the first days of treatment of severe anemia</td><td>Completed today</td><td>4.3 mmol/L, the check planned after the missed early potassium</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Methylmalonic acid and homocysteine</td><td>One week to three months after starting replacement</td><td>Due</td><td>At the eight-week review</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Injection plan: doses given, self-injection technique, site problems</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seven loading doses and three weekly self-injections given; technique good</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Neurological examination: vibration, joint position, reflexes, Romberg, gait, cognition</td><td>At diagnosis and at follow-up visits for six weeks to three months, then until recovery levels off</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Joint position sense recovered at the great toes; vibration still absent at the ankles; Romberg sway</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Falls and gait: falls since the last visit, Timed Up and Go, home hazards</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No falls; Timed Up and Go 12 seconds, from 14</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Upper endoscopy with Sydney-protocol biopsies for autoimmune gastritis, neuroendocrine tumors and dysplasia</td><td>At diagnosis of pernicious anemia; then by individual risk</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Done this morning with Sydney-protocol biopsies; body mucosa pale and thin, antrum normal, no polyp or mass; colonoscopy without polyps</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Feet: daily skin checks, footwear, callus and stump skin, monofilament</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Blister healed; daily checks kept; new extra-depth shoes</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Podiatry</td><td>Within four weeks of intake, then every three months</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen twelve days ago, three weeks after intake, after the blister; callus pared and shoes fitted; next visit in three months</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Physical therapy for balance and gait</td><td>From diagnosis until balance recovers</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Started three weeks ago, twice a week</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Find the cause of the anemia and the numb feet. Outcome by 1919-01-21: Pernicious anemia confirmed: intrinsic factor and parietal cell antibodies positive, gastrin high [S]</item><item>Normal hemoglobin and MCV within eight weeks. In progress; target 1919-03-10 [S]</item><item>Recover feeling and balance in the feet and walk without falls. In progress; target 1919-10-27 [S]</item><item>No foot wound that goes unnoticed. In progress; target 1919-10-27 [S]</item><item>Gastric surveillance planned with him. In progress; target 1919-03-10 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Guidelines for the diagnosis and treatment of cobalamin and folate disorders (2014); Vitamin B12 deficiency in over 16s: diagnosis and management (NG239) (2024); AGA Clinical Practice Update on the Diagnosis and Management of Atrophic Gastritis: Expert Review (2021); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, anemia and depression, bipolar, and mood disorders monographs (2026); Guidelines on the prevention of foot ulcers in persons with diabetes (IWGDF 2023 update) (2023); Falls Prevention in Community-Dwelling Older Adults: Interventions (2024); Colorectal Cancer: Screening (2021)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Eight-week review in three weeks with the blood tests and the biopsy results. Continue weekly injections. Next scheduled visit 1919-03-10: eight-week review: blood count, methylmalonic acid, iron studies, the endoscopy biopsies and surveillance. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
